    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_9b76ad1a95dc954effd3bbacdadf5615dc32acd125112d0d2b2447586242ff9c",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "The span supports a deliberately sparse dependency map: the theorem uses the Halasz–Montgomery inequality and three auxiliary lemmas, but the only internal connection it states is that Lemma 2's elementary inequality enables use of Lemma 3's mean-value estimates for partial sums of L-series. Treating that connection as an interface isolates where an elementary step makes a later analytic input usable; it does not establish what Lemma 1 or Halasz–Montgomery does, nor that any component is replaceable. This representation gains a visible proof dependency while losing the lemma statements, composition hypotheses, constants, and exact algebra, all of which remain part of the proof burden.",
