    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_bf7e343dfd6a414af3f973f3776bddef73ff2132205014d41a82e014a21d0e97",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "The passage transfers estimate (1.2) to estimate (1.4) through four stated moves: specialize the modulus parameter to one, apply Huxley's subdivision of the points, multiply the resulting estimate by the stated subdivision factor, and optimize an auxiliary parameter. Conceptually, this is a specialization–subdivision–correction–optimization pipeline: subdivision changes the representation of the point family, and the multiplier records a correction attached to that change before optimization. The source does not explain the subdivision geometry or make the optimization tradeoff readable, so the pipeline gains a route from one numbered estimate to another but loses quantitative factors, admissibility conditions, and the optimal choice; those remain proof obligations requiring the scan or cited method.",
